{"kind":"report","text":"Approved: exact OPS-2 head 90277b9ebbfcca56446ed46013ccd24f7e5cc530 on builder/ops2-sandbox-deploy-20261112, all 8 artifacts, for request 60f5b713 under promise b5ad29e6 and review request dcff5dd0. The head does what 60f5b713 asks, as ruling c7585e08 revises it.\n\nWhat I read:\n\n- One name table. `scripts/deploy-config.mjs` maps each of the five names to its configuration path and says whether it owns Durable Objects. `sandbox` reads `apps/sandbox/wrangler.rollout.jsonc`. `hostedNames` feeds both usage lines.\n- Migrations. For a name without Durable Objects, `checkMigrations` refuses a previous or current configuration that declares `migrations` or `durable_objects`, and it returns no tags. The room Workers keep the old check.\n- Deploy. The sandbox uses the same pinned wrangler, `--keep-vars`, dry-run bundle and receipt form as the other four.\n- The skip, under c7585e08 item 1. A previous receipt now must carry a `bundle.sha256` digest. When that digest and `configSha256` both equal the current build, `deployMain` prints the name as `unchanged` with the receipt path, sends nothing and writes no receipt for it, and continues with the other names. The rule covers all five names. A run that starts from `--deployed` has no receipt and never skips. `SCREEN_HOSTS` lives in the tracked file, so a mapping change changes the configuration bytes and deploys.\n- Smoke. `smokeSandbox` requires `--host` to be one of the screen origins in the tracked `SCREEN_HOSTS` and `--room` to be a scope ID, and it checks both before any fetch. It checks 404 for `/`, `/site/x` and `/applications/x` on the mapped host. It checks 403 with exactly `{\"ok\":false,\"reason\":\"forbidden\"}` for `/v1/scopes/\u003croom\u003e`, which only the bound room Worker can answer. It checks 404 for `/` on `unmapped-smoke.\u003cparent\u003e`, and it refuses to run if that host is itself a tracked mapping, under c7585e08 item 2. Source names no room.\n- `response` now reads a bounded JSON body only for one expected status. The room Worker smoke passes 200 where it passed `true`, so its checks are unchanged.\n- `docs/operate-a-service.md` names five Workers and one command, deploys the sandbox last because it calls the room Workers, and describes the skip and the sandbox smoke.\n\nWhat I checked:\n\n- Scope. Main 28aa0446 is the base, and the pushed branch equals the head. The builder's worktree is clean at the head. Against 28aa0446, the head changes nine paths. The 8 artifacts cover eight of them, all at the head, none stale or retired. The ninth, `packages/mcp/test/sqlite-custody.test.ts`, equals main 9e3d6673 byte for byte. The head merges onto 9e3d6673 with no conflict, so the landing changes only the eight reviewed paths. From fea6ab4d to the head, only the note and the manifest change.\n- Cheap checks. `check-notes` reports 0 warnings. The commit-message check passes: 5 checked, 0 flagged. `git diff --check` is clean.\n- Tests, on my own extracted copy. `node --test scripts/deploy.test.mjs scripts/deploy-smoke.test.mjs` passes 22 of 22.\n- Gate. The gate at fea6ab4d exited 0 (END 61303c04). The interrupted gate at the same head stays recorded with exit 130.\n- Evidence. All 19 files in the manifest match their SHA-256 values in `/Users/hughpyle/play/artroom-evidence`.\n- CI. No Card or Page path changes, so no browser run applies.\n- No deployment or live smoke ran. The planner runs the first sandbox deployment from `--deployed sandbox=221e11c3…`.\n\nArchitecture: sound. One table owns the names, paths and migration policy, and the sandbox reuses the same deploy and receipt path as the room Workers.\n\nSecurity: no finding. The smoke sends no credential, follows no redirect and reads a bounded body. It accepts only a host from the tracked file. A skipped name sends nothing.\n\nSimplification: no required change. One note: `deploySmokeMain` now has two argument parsers, one for `sandbox` and one for the room Workers. One parser that reads the name first would hold both.\n\nThis approval authorizes no landing or deployment. The planner decides the landing and runs the first sandbox deployment.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:58130d02cf03055a59fa0ec97626f2ce879d0572","binding":"assigned","head":"90277b9ebbfcca56446ed46013ccd24f7e5cc530","head_news_acknowledged":"[\"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:e95e06c792541fd9918e24c8bf6bf117bd18f0c0\"]","implementations":"[\"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:b5ad29e68c27963dbec522c097840438d5e78c38\"]","review_frontier":"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:e95e06c792541fd9918e24c8bf6bf117bd18f0c0","review_path":"reviewguard@1","verdict":"approved"}}